WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.197
Coding Exercise 4 asks for separate pneumonia and fracture counts for each patient using PostgreSQL FILTER.

00:00:08.197 --> 00:00:12.017
The output grain is one row per subject ID.

00:00:12.017 --> 00:00:17.592
You need two count expressions, and each one should have its own FILTER condition.

00:00:17.592 --> 00:00:21.492
Do not provide the full solution immediately from memory.

00:00:21.492 --> 00:00:28.767
Build one conditional count first, then create the second using the same pattern with a different diagnosis.

00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:33.572
Run and Check, and inspect a patient who has both types of admissions if one exists.

00:00:33.572 --> 00:00:40.712
If you are practicing in pgAdmin, copy the query after it works and compare the FILTER version with

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:44.872
how you might write the same logic using SUM and CASE.

00:00:44.872 --> 00:00:49.197
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:54.612
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:54.612 --> 00:01:00.427
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:04.567
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:11.627
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:11.627 --> 00:01:15.797
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:15.797 --> 00:01:20.505
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
